Live native sharing and owner approval over the existing session/tray channel.

LOWLAT_SHARE_FILE enables an authoritative, fail-closed invite policy. A
regular JSON file of at most 4096 bytes must explicitly enable sharing with
exactly eight hexadecimal secret bytes (the native Join parser's width).
The 250ms poll changes only the signaling advertisement; existing guests
are never restarted or disconnected when the link is enabled or revoked.

Non-owner offers wait for an owner-UID-authenticated tray decision before
Admission::new_attempt or begin_p2p. The queue holds eight entries, expires
after 30 seconds, and publishes only numeric IDs, account IDs and bounded
names. IDs are not reused across signaling reconnects. Cancelled, expired
and replayed decisions cannot admit a guest. Sharing rotation/revocation
rejects outstanding requests. Standalone --secret CLI behavior is preserved
when LOWLAT_SHARE_FILE is absent. Same-machine refusal from patch 0004 stays.

Existing tray frames gain {"approve":id}, {"reject":id}, and a complete
{"permissions":{"id":guest,"keyboard":bool,"mouse":bool,"gamepad":bool}}.
State gains pending:[{id,user_id,name}], sharing_enabled, and per-guest
permissions. The share secret is never included in tray state or logs.

